From **November 5, 2026**, Appwrite Cloud deletes inactive Functions and Sites deployments after **30 days** by default, which frees the project storage they use. The active deployment of every function and site is never deleted, and you can keep inactive deployments for longer by setting your own [retention window](/docs/products/functions/deployments#deployment-retention).

Appwrite creates a new deployment for your function or site every time you push to Git or redeploy. Each deployment keeps its source code and build output in your project, but only one of them is active at a time. Inactive deployments still count toward your plan's storage, and many Free projects reach their **2GB** storage limit because of them. With a 30-day default, that storage holds the files and data your app uses.

# Deployment retention on each plan

Each plan has a default retention, which applies to every function and site that doesn't set its own, and a maximum retention you can choose. On the Free and Education plans, each function or site also keeps its **100** newest inactive deployments.

| Plan | Default retention | Longest retention you can set | Inactive deployments kept per function or site |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Free and Education | 30 days | 90 days | 100 newest |
| Start and Pro | 30 days | 3 years (1,095 days) | No limit |

Enterprise plans start from the Pro values. If your organization needs a longer retention window or a custom policy, [contact the Appwrite team](/sales).

When a function or site on the Free or Education plan has more than **100** inactive deployments, Appwrite deletes the oldest ones, even inside the retention window. Deployments that are still building or sit behind a custom domain don't count toward the 100.

# How existing retention settings carry over

On November 5, your current settings carry over as follows:

- **Keep deployments forever:** functions and sites with this setting, which is the default today, move to the 30-day default. From then on, a retention of `0`, which the Console shows as **Keep deployments forever**, applies the plan default.
- **Your own retention window:** stays as it is if it's within your plan's maximum. A longer window is shortened to the maximum, so a Free function set to **1 year** keeps inactive deployments for **90 days**.

# Deployments Appwrite always keeps

Retention never deletes these deployments, however old they are:

- **The active deployment** of a function or site.
- **Deployments behind a custom domain**, such as a domain pinned to a Git branch, for as long as the domain points to them.

When Appwrite deletes an inactive deployment, it also removes the deployment's source code, build output, and preview URL.

A deployment's age counts from the day it was created, not from the day it stopped being active. If a site keeps the same active deployment for **six months**, that deployment is already past the 30-day window. When a new deployment replaces it, Appwrite deletes it at the next cleanup. To keep a deployment like that, set a longer retention window on the Start or Pro plans, or download it before you replace it.

# Keep deployments for longer

To keep inactive deployments for longer than 30 days, set a retention window before November 5. In the Appwrite Console, open your function or site and go to **Settings** > **Build** > **Retention**. Turn off **Keep deployments forever**, choose a **Retention period** up to your plan's maximum, and click **Update**.

You can also set `deploymentRetention` to a number of days with a Server SDK. Update calls replace every setting you leave out, so pass `deploymentRetention` on each update, or the function or site returns to the 30-day default. From November 5, Appwrite rejects a value above your plan's maximum with a `deployment_retention_exceeded` error.

To keep a copy of a deployment beyond your plan's window, download it before Appwrite deletes it. On the **Deployments** tab of your function or site, open the deployment's menu and choose **Download** > **Source code** or **Build output**.

If 30 days of deployment history covers your rollbacks, you don't need to do anything. On November 5, Appwrite starts deleting inactive deployments past the 30-day window, and the storage they use is freed in your project.

# Self-hosted Appwrite is unchanged

Deployment retention on self-hosted Appwrite stays off by default. You can set any window from `0`, which keeps deployments forever, to `36500` days for each function or site.

